</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Results on synthetic tasks: palindrome, multi query associative recall, and the state tracking.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Palindrome</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Palindrome requires the model to reproduce a given sequence of random tokens in reverse order.
As illustrated in Table <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Palindrome ‣ 5.1 Synthetic tests ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Kimi Linear:An Expressive, Efficient Attention Architecture" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, given an input like “<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">O G R S U N E</span>”, the model must generate its exact reversal.
Such copying tasks are known to be difficult for linear attention models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, as they struggle to precisely retrieve the entire history from a compressed, fixed-size memory state.</p>
<table id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
