</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi Query Associative Recall (MQAR)</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MQAR assesses the model’s ability to retrieve values associated with multiple queries that appear at various positions within the context.
For instance, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" title="Multi Query Associative Recall (MQAR) ‣ 5.1 Synthetic tests ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Kimi Linear:An Expressive, Efficient Attention Architecture" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the model is asked to recall <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B92622;">0</span> for the query <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B92622;">B</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#005C7F;">5</span> for <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#005C7F;">G</span>.
This task is known to be highly correlated with language modeling performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span></td>
<td id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">A</td>
<td id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">C</td>
